WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.190 align:start position:0%
 
Oh  many of us probably have fond memories  of pillow forts with big no girls  allowed signs or a treehouse labeled  princesses only or a college fraternity  full of secret handshakes and code words  but what you might not know is that  these exclusive clubs are still alive  and well in the professional world don't  get too excited though because to get  into these clubs it's not enough to  think girls are icky you've got to have  some serious social clout or very deep  pockets and sometimes that's still not  enough let's take a look at some of the  most exclusive clubs in the world  we'll start with arguably the least  difficult group to join deep inside  Disneyland in Los Angeles the secretive  club 33 caters to those with heavy  pocketbooks and a tolerance for a long  wait the wait list to join club 33 can  often exceed 12 years and once you've  waited that long you get the privilege  of paying a $25,000 initiation fee and a  further 12 thousand dollars in annual  dues pretty steep so what does a $25,000  ticket buy you for starters  you get access to a private restaurant  the only place in the entire Magic  Kingdom that serves alcohol you'll also  get behind-the-scenes access to all  things Disneyland fast passes valet  parking on a private lot automatic  upgrades at any Disney property reserved  seating at shows a private car on the  Disney Train access to special events  and reservations for friends and family  for those who see the appeal of  Disneyland and its lifestyle the  initiation fee and annual dues may be  worth it but for the not so die-hard  Disney fans it's hard to justify $25,000  on amusement park perks if you're more  interested in rubbing elbows with rich  and famous creative types core maybe  more your style at this swanky Oasis in  New York City you'll have access to a  gym a spa art galleries individualized  service a satellite office amazing food  and drinks and a fleet of rental sports  cars Corps also has its own movie  theater where stars like George Clooney  have hosted screenings of their new  films and guest speakers like Dave  Chappelle make frequent visits big names  from Kenneth Cole to Roger Waters of  Pink Floyd have been known to hang out  at the club and enjoy some of the finest  luxuries New York has to offer then  there's the price tag the initiation fee  is a whopping fifty thousand dollars  with annual dues of fifteen thousand  more per year while core may sound more  exciting than club 33 to most people  whether it's twenty five thousand  dollars more  is another question if the city  lifestyle isn't for you there's the  Hermitage Club nestled in the pristine  mountains of West Dover Vermont the  Hermitage has everything the affluent  outdoor lover could ever need  1,400 acres of ski slopes a year-round  ski lodge with its own five-star hotel  an 18-hole golf course fly-fishing  bowling concerts by big name artists  like Hall &amp; Oates and an 80,000 square  foot Clubhouse to sit back and unwind  from a day on the slopes  if this sounds ideal to you just grab  your checkbook and send over a cool  $75,000 initiation fee and prepare for  an additional sixty five hundred dollars  of annual dues on the plus side the  seventy five thousand does cover a whole  family not just one person so really  it's a bargain okay I get it  enough with these cheap clubs you don't  want to associate with the peasants one  of the very finest groups one can buy  into is the prestigious Yellowstone Club  a 13,000 600 acre private ski and Golf  Club and residential community located  in beautiful Big Sky Montana as of 2009  the minimum fee to join the club was a  staggering two hundred and fifty  thousand dollars with an additional  twenty thousand in annual dues okay so  what that's pocket change not so fast  you also have to own a home in the  vicinity which will set you back  anywhere from two to over twenty million  dollars members of the Yellowstone Club  have included Bill Gates LA Dodgers  owner Frank McCourt former Vice  President Dan Quayle businessman Peter  Chernin  and CEO of NBC Universal Steve Burke if  hanging out on the slopes with some of  the wealthiest people on the planet  sounds like the life for you then the  Yellowstone Club is the only way to go  of course there are some clubs that no  amount of money will get you into one  such Club is the mysterious Bilderberg  Group an annual invite-only three-day  conference of about a hundred and fifty  leaders and notable figures from  industry finance academia and the media  according to the Bilderberg meetings  website the conference is a forum for  informal discussions about mega trends  and major issues facing the world and  hosts some of the most influential North  American and Western Europeans currently  living in order to get into this club  you'd have to hold a prominent position  in a global company or institution of  higher learning or somehow contribute to  the global economy on a large scale  the current steering committee the group  responsible for setting annual topics  and event  includes members from microsoft goldman  sachs deutsche bank TD bank and airbus  the super secretive Bilderberg Group has  inevitably sparked some conspiracy  theory since its inception including the  idea that they intend to establish a  global New World Order but proponents of  the group maintained that it was  instrumental in the formation of the  European Union these are just a sampling  of the many hyper exclusive clubs and  organizations that exist around the  world today if you have the social  status or pockets deep enough to join  one of these groups congratulations you  officially have access to things only a  very tiny portion of society could even  dream of so go ahead and put in a good  word for me if you enjoyed this video  consider subscribing to support the  channel and click the little bell to  join the notification squad you can  watch my previous video by clicking here  or if you're really bored you can binge  watch all my content by clicking here  thanks for watching and I'll see you  next week

